TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "player absence",
    "offensive reorganization",
    "pre-absence initiation profile",
    "teammate similarity (profile, minutes, initiations, same-position minutes)",
    "primary play types of absent player",
    "largest gainer in usage share"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define pre-absence initiation profile (play-type frequency vector)?",
    "How to measure 'closest' profile: Euclidean distance, cosine similarity, or rank-based?",
    "How to handle multiple similarity criteria (minutes, initiations, same-position minutes) simultaneously?",
    "How to define and aggregate the absent player's primary types (top N, threshold)?",
    "How to measure 'gain' in those types: absolute change in possession count, share, or points?",
    "What is the unit of analysis: absence spell (n=148) or distinct absent player (n=11)?",
    "How to account for within-player and within-team clustering over multiple spells?",
    "How to control for concurrent injuries, schedule, opponent, and game script?",
    "How to guard against regression-to-the-mean in gains for the most similar teammate?",
    "How to handle absence spells of varying length and partial-game returns?",
    "What is the appropriate multiple-comparison correction across play types and similarity metrics?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No previous work by this project on the hypothesis is available; the question has not been tested or pre-registered."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question is a highly structured causal claim: that the teammate most similar in offensive role absorbs the majority of the absent player's specific play-type demands.",
    "Operationalizing 'closest pre-absence initiation profile' requires defining an initiation vector per player per game, a pre-absence baseline window, and a distance metric; without pre-registration this yields researcher degrees of freedom.",
    "The data contain play-by-play derived initiator-play-type assignments, making technical measurement possible; however play-type classification itself is subject to noise and boundary decisions.",
    "The sample of only 11 distinct absent players severely limits statistical power and prevents valid generalization; effective degrees of freedom collapse when clustering on player.",
    "Even with 148 spells, the 'closest teammate' is often the same across spells for a given absent player, reducing independent comparisons.",
    "Gain in usage of primary types is mechanically bounded (ceiling effects) and subject to regression to the mean because the most similar teammate already has a high base-rate in those types.",
    "Absence events are non-random (injury/rest), which can systematically bias which teammates benefit, e.g., if the absent star's absence coincides with a schedule of weak opponents.",
    "Multiple-confounder control (concurrent absences, opponent defensive scheme, pace) is essential but difficult with 11 clusters and many covariates.",
    "The hypothesis would require testing across several dimensions (minutes vs. initiations vs. same-position minutes) and multiple play-type definitions, inflating multiplicity risk."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Exact operational definition of 'initiation profile' – e.g., per-100-poss session frequencies with regularization for sparse play types.",
    "Definition of 'primary types': how many types, what minimum frequency, and whether secondary types are considered.",
    "Whether 'closest' should be defined by a single metric or a combination; how to handle ties or near-ties.",
    "How spells are delimited (games missed, minutes absent) and whether partial injury returns are included.",
    "The precise meaning of 'n=148; 148 spells; 11 cases' – whether it refers to 148 absence spells from 11 unique absent players, or some other aggregation."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No prior study records were retrieved, so the decision is based solely on the feasibility assessment of the current data."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.15,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Only 11 distinct absent players; even 148 spells are unlikely to provide independent observations after clustering. Power to detect a moderate effect is very low."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Some play types may be rare, and the 'primary types' of an absent player may not shift meaningfully to any teammate if offensive schemes change completely."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Play-type initiation classification from tracking? Actually from play-by-play; derived assignment of initiator and play type is subject to algorithmic noise, and distance between initiation profiles inherits that noise."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "The teammate with closest profile is likely already above the team mean in those play types; random fluctuation in the pre-absence period will regress to the mean during absence, creating a spurious gain."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Absence timing (injury/rest, back-to-backs, opponent strength, presence of other injuries) is strongly confounded with which teammates see increased usage."
      },
      {
        "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
        "text": "Several candidate similarity definitions (minutes, initiations, position) and an expandable set of 'primary types' create a multiple-comparison problem not easily corrected with small cluster count."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No earlier project studies were retrieved that tested the hypothesis or addressed its feasibility."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The effective sample size (11 unique absent players) is far below rule-of-thumb thresholds for either fixed- or random-effects inference on player-level replacement patterns."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "A single player's absence may generate zero or one genuinely comparable 'closest' teammate; many absence spells may produce no interpretable gain event."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "No validated operational definition of initiation-profile distance exists in this project; ad-hoc choices can arbitrarily reverse the ranking of 'closest' teammate."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Selecting the 'closest' based on pre-absence data creates a regression-to-the-mean trap in the observed gains."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Opponent defensive strength, game pace, home/road, and simultaneous absence of other rotation players are unlikely to be convincingly controlled in a sample of 11 absent players."
    },
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "Classifying a play type as 'primary' for the absent player and as a 'gain' for a teammate involves thresholds that can be set post-hoc."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing multiple similarity definitions, multiple time windows, and multiple 'gain' metrics without pre-specification inflates false positive rates."
    },
    {
      "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
      "text": "Usage rate is bounded above; a teammate already near the maximum cannot gain further, masking the hypothesized relationship in high-usage absent players."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.05,
    "untestable": 0.5,
    "artifact": 0.2,
    "uncertain": 0.2
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "For each absence spell i, let j be the teammate with the smallest pre-absence initiation-profile distance to absent player k (Euclidean distance on per-100-poss frequency vectors of 11 play types). The estimand is the ratio of j's post- to pre-absence possession share in k's primary types (defined as types where k exceeded the 75th percentile of team average frequency) divided by the analogous ratio for the non-j teammates, averaged across spells, weighting by spell length.",
    "unit": "Absence spell, but with primary analysis clustered on absent player k (11 clusters); sensitivity analysis aggregating to one observation per k using the within-k average or the spell with largest absence length.",
    "sample": "148 spells from 11 distinct absent players, sourced from 2022‑23 through 2025‑26 NBA play-by-play derived play-type records. Pre-absence window: last 15 team games with the player active before the first missed game.",
    "power": "With 11 clusters and an estimated intra-class correlation of 0.3–0.5, a medium-large effect size (Cohen's d ≈ 0.8) would be detectable with ~40% power at α = 0.05 (two-sided). The sample is insufficient to detect smaller, realistic effects.",
    "controls": "Pre-specified list: opponent defensive rating, pace, home/away, back-to-back indicator, concurrent absence count for the team, and a linear term for the share of the absent player's possessions handled by the closest-profile teammate in the pre-absence period.",
    "falsification": "(a) The gain should disappear if the 'closest' teammate is chosen by a different metric (minutes, initiations, same-position minutes) that is not the profile distance; (b) the ratio should be indistinguishable from 1 for play types that are not primary for the absent player (placebo test).",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-register a single primary similarity metric (Euclidean distance on 11-type vectors) and a single definition of primary types (types with absent-player frequency above 75th percentile of team pre-absence average). All alternative metrics reported as robustness analyses with a pre-specified order and no hypothesis test.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject null hypothesis of no differential gain (ratio = 1) if the cluster-robust 95% confidence interval excludes 1 and the falsification tests show null effects (ratio not different from 1 for non-primary types). Require p < 0.01 for any secondary specification to be considered suggestive.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022–23 through 2025–26 play-by-play and derived play-type possession records as of 2026-11-01."
  }
}

